How It Works

The 8-puzzle is represented as a tuple of 9 numbers, where 0 is the blank tile. The start state is (1, 2, 3, 4, 0, 5, 6, 7, 8) and the goal state is (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 0).

get_neighbors() returns every state reachable by sliding one tile into the blank (Up, Down, Left, Right).
solve_bfs() explores the puzzle level by level using a FIFO queue (collections.deque), so it always finds the goal at the shallowest depth.
solve_dfs() explores the puzzle branch by branch using a LIFO stack, going as deep as possible before backtracking.
Both functions keep a visited set so no state is expanded twice, and both count every node they expand.
run_experiments() runs each solver 3 times, times each run, and prints the average time along with the node counts.
Results

On the start/goal pair above, BFS expanded far fewer nodes than DFS and finished much faster, because it stops as soon as it reaches the goal at the shallowest depth. DFS explored a much larger share of the state space before happening to reach the goal. This matches the theory: BFS is complete and optimal when every move costs the same, while DFS is not.

